# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# path/src/imported-tuning.json — MAIN'S COPY ALWAYS WINS A MERGE.
#
# The file is 453KB across 186 sections and it is the shipped look of the game,
# not scratch: config.js imports it on line 1 and saved tuning beats config.js
# in the merge, so whatever is in here IS what players see.
#
# It is also machine-written. The ` tuner POSTs to /__tuning and rewrites the
# whole snapshot while the game runs, so any branch left open for a few days
# carries a stale copy of it forward without anyone editing a thing. Merging
# that branch replays week-old sliders over current work — the game visibly
# changes and the diff is 10,000 lines of noise nobody reads. Four of the
# worktrees harvested on 2026-09-18 were carrying exactly that.
#
# `ours` is right rather than a real 3-way merge because tuning is authored in
# ONE place (the main tree, with the game running) and nowhere else. A branch
# has no tuning worth keeping; it only has an older photograph of main's.
#
# THIS LINE DOES NOTHING ON ITS OWN. There is no built-in `ours` merge driver —
# with the attribute set and no driver defined, git falls straight through to a
# normal 3-way merge and conflicts, which is indistinguishable from never
# having written this file. The driver has to exist in git config:
#
#     git config merge.ours.driver true
#
# `npm run mergedriver` does that, `npm install` runs it via `prepare`, and
# `npm run test:mergeguard` fails if it is missing — because a protection that
# silently is not there is worse than none.
path/src/imported-tuning.json merge=ours

# The same applies to the backup snapshots the tuner leaves beside it.
path/src/imported-tuning.backup-*.json merge=ours
